TL;DR: the "tau" program's documentation, and as much of its code as is
attributable to xedakini, is hereby placed under the 0BSD License[1].
The copyright licensing status of any vestiges of the C code written by
Jason Stratos Papadopoulos are less clear.


This code is at least morally, and *maybe* even legally, a derivative
work of code written by Jason Stratos Papadopoulos and published to the
Usenet group comp.lang.c in 1997:
   https://groups.google.com/g/comp.lang.c/c/PsilyZ9LNGA/m/twL_M4dRIoMJ

The included "tau.c" file, though tweaked by me, I consider to be
fully derivative of the original comp.lang.c posting.  It can serve
as a reference for anyone trying to decide how much of the Rust code
is derivative vs. how much is novel.


IANAL, and I am unclear on how much this rewrite in Rust is considered
"derivative" and how much is considered "independent expression".
The rewrite in Rust is much more radical than a mere mechanical translation
from the C, but Mr. Papadopoulos's code definitely served as inspiration as
to how to approach the problem of computing atan(1) to extended precision.
[The distinction of Papadopoulos's being used to compute pi, rather than
this code's preference for computing tau, is not relevant, as making
this distinction involves only a trivial tweak in the code.]

To the extent that this code is derivative, the licensing situation is
somewhat ambiguous: posting to comp.lang.c certainly implied a certain
amount of intent that the code could be copied and used; the posting
also included a declaration of:
    "give it to anyone you want, I don't care".
This isn't quite an "Unlicense" or "WTFPL" declaration, but it seems to nod
in that direction.  Personally, I've been treating it as-if it were a 0BSD
license, with the additional constraint that credit must be given.

To the extent that code here _is_ considered original to me, I release
_my_ contribution(s) under the 0BSD License; to the extent that this
code is considered derivative, one will have to decide for oneself what
aspects of the code are derivative of Mr. Papadopoulos's code, and how
his declaration may or may not inhibit your use of said code.


